<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements embedded in Uttrflow.app's signature by Scripts/bundle.sh.

  One entitlement. An entitlement widens what the app is permitted to do, so the list is
  kept to what has been shown to be necessary, and what is absent is recorded below with
  the reason, because "we never needed it" is only useful if someone wrote down how they
  know.

  com.apple.security.device.audio-input — REQUIRED for distribution.

  Measured on macOS 26.5.1, same binary, same bundle identifier, hardened runtime the
  only variable (Docs/packaging.md has the runs). On a Mac that has not already granted
  this identifier the microphone, a hardened build WITHOUT this entitlement does not
  prompt and does not error: AVCaptureDevice.requestAccess returns false immediately,
  the audio engine starts, buffers arrive, and every sample is exactly 0.0. macOS writes
  no TCC record at all. From inside the app, and from the user's chair, that is
  indistinguishable from having pressed Deny.

  With the entitlement present the hardened runtime changes nothing: the prompt appears,
  the grant is recorded, real audio arrives.

  An already-granted Mac keeps working either way, because the check is skipped once TCC
  has an answer — which is precisely why this cannot be caught by testing on the machine
  that built the app. Scripts/bundle.sh check 5 and Scripts/notarise.sh both refuse a
  build that has lost it.

  Deliberately absent, each verified unnecessary by running the app under the hardened
  runtime with library validation enforced (CoreML, the Neural Engine and Metal all
  initialise; see Docs/packaging.md):

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
        Still absent, but no longer for the reason first written here. That reason was
        that the app links nothing but system frameworks and /usr/lib/swift dylibs,
        because SwiftPM statically links the package graph. Sparkle ended that: it is an
        embedded framework in Contents/Frameworks, and it is the first thing here that
        library validation has an opinion about.

        It is absent because with a Developer ID it is not needed. Library validation
        requires that loaded code share the app's Team ID, and a distribution build signs
        the app and every nested piece of Sparkle with the same one — so the check passes
        on its merits. Adding this would let any library signed by anyone load into a
        process holding microphone and Accessibility access, which is a permanent cost to
        buy nothing.

        Ad-hoc signatures have no Team ID at all, so an ad-hoc *hardened* build cannot
        load the framework — it dies at launch with "different Team IDs". That is the
        `rehearsal` mode in Scripts/bundle.sh, and only that mode, which signs with this
        entitlement added to a copy of this file and says so while it does it. Check 7
        refuses a distribution build that carries it.
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
        Whisper and the local model compile through CoreML and Metal, both of which do
        that work in separate system services rather than in our address space.
    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
        Nothing here needs DYLD_* set, and honouring it is how a hardened process gets
        code injected into it.
    com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
        Uttrflow types into other applications with CGEvent and reads context through the
        Accessibility API. Neither is an Apple Event. Accessibility has no
        hardened-runtime entitlement at all — it is governed only by TCC — so there is
        nothing here that could be missing.
    com.apple.security.app-sandbox
        Uttrflow is not sandboxed. Note that audio-input above is nominally a sandbox
        entitlement; the hardened runtime consults it regardless, which is the whole
        trap described above.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
